  "task_id": "LEV-CH01-RIEMANN-RECTANGLE-INTERFACE-PRODUCTION-20260908",
  "source_sha256": "b3adec0d3616dbde57a5522cfce1861890887d7c03a2232d2136cb94c9bac1d5",
  "source_evidence": [
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1; equation (1.1) and following paragraph",
      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + A q_x(x,t) = 0; q : R × R → R^m",
      "observation": "The inherited setting is a time-dependent, one-dimensional system with an m-component real state; in the constant-coefficient linear case A is a constant real m-by-m matrix."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "A has real eigenvalues and a corresponding set of m linearly independent eigenvectors",
      "observation": "Hyperbolicity of the constant-coefficient system means a complete set of real characteristic speeds and independent eigenvectors."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; section 1.1, equations (1.8)–(1.9)",
      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + f(q(x,t))_x = 0",
      "observation": "The conservation law uses a flux function f of the state q. Its quasilinear expression is q_t + f'(q)q_x = 0; hyperbolicity is associated with the previously stated matrix conditions for the flux Jacobian."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4; section 1.1.1, equation (1.10)",
      "anchor": "for any two points x_1 and x_2",
      "observation": "The integral conservation law states d/dt ∫ from x_1 to x_2 q(x,t) dx = f(q(x_1,t)) − f(q(x_2,t)). Each component of q measures a conserved density, and its total amount changes only through endpoint fluxes."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4; section 1.1.2, opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "provided that q and f(q) are sufficiently smooth",
      "observation": "Derivation of the differential conservation law from the integral law requires sufficient smoothness. Interesting solutions may instead contain discontinuities such as shock waves."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; continuation of section 1.1.2",
      "anchor": "the integral conservation law (1.10) is the more fundamental equation which does continue to hold",
      "observation": "Discontinuities may develop from smooth initial data. At a discontinuity the differential equation does not hold classically, whereas the source asserts continued validity of the integral conservation law."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; section 1.2, first paragraph",
      "anchor": "the cell average of q, which is this integral divided by the volume of the cell",
      "observation": "Finite volume methods partition the domain into grid cells and approximate cell integrals, or more specifically normalized cell averages. Flux through cell edges modifies these values each time step; numerical flux functions approximate the correct fluxes using approximate cell averages."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; section 1.2.1, opening paragraph and equation (1.11)",
      "anchor": "q(x,0) = q_l if x < 0; q_r if x > 0",
      "observation": "A Riemann problem consists of the hyperbolic equation with piecewise constant initial data having a single jump, placed at x = 0 for the displayed formula."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; paragraph following equation (1.11)",
      "anchor": "q_l = Q_{i−1} and q_r = Q_i",
      "observation": "Cell averages in two neighboring cells supply the left and right Riemann states. Solving this problem yields information usable to compute a numerical flux and update cell averages over a time step."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; paragraph following equation (1.11)",
      "anchor": "typically a similarity solution, a function of x/t alone",
      "observation": "The source describes a typical hyperbolic Riemann solution as a finite collection of waves propagating from the origin with constant wave speeds. It also states that linear hyperbolic Riemann problems are solved in terms of the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of A."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "A state q depending on one spatial variable x and time t, with values in R^m in the inherited system setting; m is the number of components. [Printed p. 1, equation (1.1) and following paragraph.]",
      "A flux function f of q, supplying the conserved flux in q_t + f(q)_x = 0. [Printed p. 3, equation (1.8).]",
      "Arbitrary spatial endpoints x_1 and x_2 in the integral balance. [Printed p. 4, section 1.1.1, introduction to equation (1.10).]",
      "A partition into grid cells, and neighboring cells indexed i−1 and i with cell averages Q_{i−1} and Q_i. The selected passage does not specify the index set. [Printed p. 5, section 1.2 and paragraph following equation (1.11).]",
      "Constant left and right states q_l and q_r, prescribed respectively on x < 0 and x > 0 at t = 0. [Printed p. 5, equation (1.11).]"
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      "The conservation-law setting is homogeneous: q_t + f(q)_x = 0. Source terms are discussed separately as necessary when the relevant quantity is not conserved. [Printed p. 3, section 1.1; printed p. 4, paragraph following equation (1.10).]",
      "Deriving the differential equation from the integral equation assumes that q and f(q) are sufficiently smooth. This assumption is attached to that derivation, not imposed on all finite volume solutions. [Printed p. 4, section 1.1.2.]",
      "The Riemann construction uses a hyperbolic equation together with piecewise constant initial data with a single jump. [Printed p. 5, section 1.2.1 and equation (1.11).]",
      "For the neighboring-cell workflow, Q_{i−1} and Q_i are cell averages in two neighboring finite volume grid cells, and the Riemann data are chosen as q_l = Q_{i−1} and q_r = Q_i. [Printed p. 5, paragraph following equation (1.11).]"
    ],
    "conclusions": [
      "For any endpoints x_1 and x_2, the integral conservation balance is d/dt ∫ from x_1 to x_2 q(x,t) dx = f(q(x_1,t)) − f(q(x_2,t)). [Printed p. 4, equation (1.10).]",
      "Discontinuities can arise from smooth initial data; at a discontinuity the differential equation does not hold classically, while the integral conservation law continues to hold according to the source. [Printed pp. 4–5, section 1.1.2.]",
      "Finite volume methods approximate cell integrals or normalized cell averages and modify the cell values each time step through edge fluxes. Numerical flux functions approximate the correct fluxes from the approximate cell averages. [Printed p. 5, section 1.2, first paragraph.]",
      "Solving the Riemann problem with the two neighboring cell averages as its left and right states provides information that can be used to compute a numerical flux and update cell averages over a time step. [Printed p. 5, paragraph following equation (1.11).]",
      "A hyperbolic Riemann solution is described as typically depending on x/t alone and consisting of finitely many waves propagating from the origin with constant wave speeds. Linear hyperbolic Riemann problems are described as solvable using the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of A. [Printed p. 5, paragraph following equation (1.11).]"
    ],
    "definitions_and_conventions": [
      "Subscripts t and x denote partial derivatives. [Printed p. 1, paragraph following equation (1.1).]",
      "Each component of q is a density of a conserved quantity; its spatial integral is the corresponding total amount. The balance has left endpoint flux minus right endpoint flux. [Printed p. 4, equation (1.10) and following paragraph.]",
      "A cell average is the integral of q over that cell divided by the cell volume; in the inherited one-dimensional setting this volume is its length. The length interpretation follows from the one-dimensional context. [Printed p. 1, introduction to equation (1.1); printed p. 5, section 1.2.]",
      "A Riemann problem includes both the hyperbolic equation and its special initial data. The origin is an illustrative placement of the jump, introduced as 'say x = 0'. [Printed p. 5, section 1.2.1.]",
      "Equation (1.11) specifies strict half-lines x < 0 and x > 0 and leaves the initial value at x = 0 unspecified. [Printed p. 5, equation (1.11).]"
    ],
    "implicit_context": [
      "The selected discussion is an introductory description of finite volume methods and Riemann problems, rather than a numbered theorem giving a fully quantified numerical algorithm. [Printed p. 5, sections 1.2–1.2.1, structure and prose.]",
      "For the constant-coefficient linear specialization, A is real and constant, and hyperbolicity requires real eigenvalues with m independent eigenvectors. [Printed p. 1, equation (1.1); printed p. 3, opening paragraph.]",
      "The differential and integral descriptions are linked through conservation, but classical differentiability is expressly unavailable at discontinuities. [Printed pp. 4–5, section 1.1.2.]"
    ],
    "semantic_facets": [
      {
        "topic": "Normalization and approximation",
        "details": [
          "The numerical quantity approximated is a normalized spatial integral, not merely an unnormalized mass. [Printed p. 5, section 1.2, cell-average definition.]",
          "The flux approximation uses approximate cell averages, identified as the available data. [Printed p. 5, section 1.2, first paragraph.]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Neighboring-cell Riemann workflow",
        "details": [
          "The lower indexed neighbor supplies the left state and cell i supplies the right state. [Printed p. 5, paragraph following equation (1.11).]",
          "The stated sequence is Riemann solve, extraction of usable information, computation of a numerical flux, and updating cell averages over a time step. [Printed p. 5, paragraph following equation (1.11).]",
          "The passage does not specify an interface sampling formula, a time-integrated numerical flux, a time-step restriction, or an explicit cell-update equation. [Printed p. 5, sections 1.2–1.2.1, displayed equation (1.11) and accompanying workflow paragraph.]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Scope of conservation across discontinuities",
        "details": [
          "Continued integral conservation is asserted for discontinuous solutions, while classical pointwise PDE validity fails at a discontinuity. [Printed p. 5, continuation of section 1.1.2.]",
          "The selected pages state an instantaneous interval balance, not an explicit space-time rectangle identity. Integrating that balance in time would be an additional analyst derivation requiring a suitable interpretation. [Printed p. 4, equation (1.10); printed p. 5, section 1.1.2.]"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "undebatable_constraints": [
    "Preserve the flux orientation f(q(x_1,t)) − f(q(x_2,t)). [Printed p. 4, equation (1.10).]",
    "Preserve division by cell volume when representing a cell average. [Printed p. 5, section 1.2.]",
    "Preserve q_l = Q_{i−1} and q_r = Q_i in the neighboring-cell construction. [Printed p. 5, paragraph following equation (1.11).]",
    "Do not require classical PDE validity at shock discontinuities or transfer the smoothness condition for the differential derivation to all discontinuous solutions. [Printed pp. 4–5, section 1.1.2.]",
    "Do not assign a source-mandated value to q(0,0); equation (1.11) specifies only strict half-lines. [Printed p. 5, equation (1.11).]",
    "Preserve the qualifier 'typically' on the similarity-solution description; the passage does not assert an unrestricted existence, uniqueness, or similarity theorem. [Printed p. 5, paragraph following equation (1.11).]",
    "Do not attribute a specific rectangle balance, numerical flux formula, or update formula to these pages. Their explicit balance is equation (1.10), and their numerical workflow is descriptive. [Printed p. 4, equation (1.10); printed p. 5, sections 1.2–1.2.1.]"
  ],
  "ambiguities": [
    {
      "issue": "The precise analytic interpretation of integral conservation at discontinuities is not specified.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 4, equation (1.10), uses a time derivative and endpoint evaluations; printed p. 5, section 1.1.2, says this law continues to hold at discontinuities without specifying traces, almost-everywhere time validity, or a weak formulation.",
      "impact": "The source does not select a precise regularity class or justify requiring a classical time derivative and unrestricted endpoint evaluations at every time."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The initial value at the jump is unspecified.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 5, equation (1.11), gives values for x < 0 and x > 0 only.",
      "impact": "Any convention at x = 0 is additional to the displayed source data."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The numerical realization of the workflow is unspecified.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 5, paragraph following equation (1.11), says Riemann-solution information can be used to compute a numerical flux and update cell averages, without a formula.",
      "impact": "A particular sampling rule, solver implementation, time interval, mesh geometry, or update identity cannot be extracted as an explicit requirement."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The similarity and wave-description scope is qualitative.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 5, paragraph following equation (1.11), uses 'typically' before the similarity-solution description.",
      "impact": "The description must not be strengthened into a universal theorem for all hyperbolic equations or interpreted as saying every wave is a single discontinuity."
    }
  ],
  "contract_plain_english": "The source asserts conservation of each spatially integrated conserved density through left endpoint flux minus right endpoint flux, and says the integral law remains fundamental for discontinuous solutions where the classical PDE fails (printed pp. 4–5, equation (1.10) and section 1.1.2). Finite volume methods approximate spatial cell averages, defined by dividing cell integrals by cell volumes, and update these values using edge fluxes estimated from approximate cell averages (printed p. 5, section 1.2). For neighboring cells, take Q_{i−1} as the left constant Riemann state and Q_i as the right constant state; solving the hyperbolic initial-value problem supplies information usable to compute a numerical flux and update cell averages over a time step (printed p. 5, equation (1.11) and following paragraph). The source leaves the jump-point value and numerical formulas unspecified, and qualifies its similarity-solution description as typical. These pages do not explicitly state a space-time rectangle theorem."
}
